WEBVTT

00:00:00.155 --> 00:00:02.352
This is someone shopping
with only a keyboard.

00:00:03.343 --> 00:00:05.393
They press Tab to move through the page…

00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:07.242
and the Add to cart button gets skipped.

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:08.599
They can't buy anything.

00:00:08.843 --> 00:00:09.618
That's not rare.

00:00:09.618 --> 00:00:15.268
In 2026, 95.9 percent
of the top million home pages

00:00:15.268 --> 00:00:16.667
had accessibility failures.

00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:19.814
And almost all of them come
from the same six mistakes.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.283
So we built Curb Cut.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:23.877
It fixes those barriers from the outside,

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:26.521
right in your browser,
and proves every fix works.

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:27.844
Step one.

00:00:27.844 --> 00:00:32.669
Kane, an AI testing agent, tries the task
the way a disabled person would.

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:33.320
It fails.

00:00:33.601 --> 00:00:35.326
Kane tries again with a mouse.

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:36.494
The same task works.

00:00:36.663 --> 00:00:38.809
So the website is broken, not the task.

00:00:39.085 --> 00:00:39.810
Step two.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.835
An agent writes a small, safe fix.

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:43.240
Just rules, no code.

00:00:43.519 --> 00:00:44.444
Step three.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:47.469
Kane runs the task again with the fix,
and it passes.

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:48.762
The recording is the proof.

00:00:49.052 --> 00:00:49.927
Step four.

00:00:49.927 --> 00:00:53.509
The extension shares the proven fix
with everyone who visits that site.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.935
Some barriers are whole journeys.

00:00:55.935 --> 00:01:00.842
On mass.edu, a keyboard user reaches About,
and the menu never opens.

00:01:01.087 --> 00:01:03.862
With Curb Cut, the same keys open the menu,

00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:05.370
and Enter takes you to the page.

00:01:05.649 --> 00:01:09.699
For blind users, the most common barrier
is a button with no name.

00:01:09.699 --> 00:01:11.494
A screen reader just says, button.

00:01:11.631 --> 00:01:15.256
On Munich Airport, Curb Cut names it:
previous slide.

00:01:15.256 --> 00:01:17.428
Kane found it with the fix,
and couldn't without it.

00:01:17.676 --> 00:01:20.676
The most common failure of all is faint text.

00:01:20.676 --> 00:01:23.726
Curb Cut nudges the colour
just enough to be readable,

00:01:23.726 --> 00:01:24.347
and measures it.

00:01:24.606 --> 00:01:28.081
And on pages we've never fixed,
there's an experiment.

00:01:28.081 --> 00:01:31.031
Jev, a fast decision model from TypeSafe,

00:01:31.031 --> 00:01:33.750
names unlabelled buttons live,
in about a second.

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:37.197
On real sites, 13 of its 15 names were right.

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:39.009
When it isn't sure, it stays quiet.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.147
On OpenSeaMap, a site we've never fixed,
the keyboard skips View.

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.330
One shortcut reports the barrier.

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:47.828
Grok writes the fix in sixteen seconds.

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.992
Kane tries it. Without the fix, the
keyboard fails.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.267
The mouse works.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.307
With the fix, the keyboard passes.

00:01:55.633 --> 00:01:59.035
Minutes later, after a recheck, the fix
ships in the extension.

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.247
Now the same eight Tabs open View.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:02.946
Sea Marks is right there.

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.967
Today, 106 fixes are live in the
extension.

00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:09.280
62 of them proven by Kane.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:11.951
And every fix can go back to the site owner:

00:02:11.951 --> 00:02:15.401
the exact change,
with Kane's recording as proof.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:18.239
When the website fixes itself,
our patch retires.

00:02:18.497 --> 00:02:19.322
Curb Cut.

00:02:19.322 --> 00:02:21.622
Fixing the web for the people it shuts out.

00:02:21.622 --> 00:02:22.823
Verified by Kane.

00:02:23.153 --> 00:02:26.193
Developers can do the same thing
on a site they're building.

00:02:26.193 --> 00:02:28.209
Start with what a visitor came to do.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:33.536
Here, someone wants to find a pair of headphones
and add them to their cart using a keyboard.

00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:37.313
Give that task and the
address to curbcut check.

00:02:37.313 --> 00:02:39.258
Kane opens the shop and tries it.

00:02:39.678 --> 00:02:40.913
Watch the Add button.

00:02:40.913 --> 00:02:42.673
The keyboard skips right past it.

00:02:42.673 --> 00:02:44.489
The headphones stay on the shelf.

00:02:45.653 --> 00:02:48.013
Curb Cut proposes a small change.

00:02:48.013 --> 00:02:51.089
Let the button take focus,
and let Enter activate it.

00:02:51.509 --> 00:02:52.793
Kane tries again.

00:02:52.793 --> 00:02:55.593
Same shop, same headphones, same keyboard.

00:02:55.593 --> 00:02:57.503
This time there's an item in the cart.

00:02:58.653 --> 00:03:01.573
A developer can put that
check into Claude Code, too.

00:03:01.573 --> 00:03:03.709
Here it runs when Claude tries to finish.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:06.843
The first time, the button
still skips the keyboard.

00:03:06.843 --> 00:03:09.804
The check fails and the Stop
hook sends the work back.

00:03:11.153 --> 00:03:13.993
The developer fixes the
button in the site itself.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.569
Claude reaches the end again.

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:18.153
This time Kane can add the headphones.

00:03:18.153 --> 00:03:20.256
The check passes and Claude can finish.

00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:25.473
A person using the extension
can look at the evidence, too.

00:03:25.473 --> 00:03:28.202
Open Curb Cut on the page
and choose the recording.

00:03:28.622 --> 00:03:30.753
It shows what happened in the browser.

00:03:30.753 --> 00:03:33.165
You can see for yourself
whether the menu opened.

00:03:34.653 --> 00:03:37.593
On a new page, Jev looks
at what each button does.

00:03:37.593 --> 00:03:40.233
Here on Meridiano, one opens the menu.

00:03:40.233 --> 00:03:41.586
Another opens search.

00:03:42.006 --> 00:03:43.913
People checked the suggestions.

00:03:43.913 --> 00:03:46.273
Jev got 13 right out of 15.

00:03:46.273 --> 00:03:48.534
When it's unsure,
it leaves the button alone.

00:03:49.653 --> 00:03:53.033
If something else gets in the
way, open Report a barrier.

00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:55.925
Describe what you were trying
to do and where you got stuck.

00:03:56.345 --> 00:03:58.914
The extension keeps a
draft so you can review it.

00:04:00.033 --> 00:04:01.545
The report lands in the queue.

00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:04.644
Grok makes View open on keyboard focus.

00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:07.271
The publisher checks Kane's saved
evidence.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.883
Open the extension to watch the
recordings.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.200
And when a site owner makes a repair,
the browser should get out of the way.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.637
Kane tries the task with the
extension's fix switched off.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.560
The button works on its own.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.337
Curb Cut retires the fix.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.428
The next person who comes to buy those
headphones can use the keyboard from the start.
